---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "shakeology protein powder",
      "shakeology review",
      "is shakeology worth it",
      "shakeology 0g added sugar",
      "is shakeology third party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Meal Replacement Shakes",
    link: "/best-meal-replacement-shake/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Shakeology Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      BODi's Shakeology is sold as a superfood shake, priced like a premium meal replacement and bought by many people as a protein powder. Here is what the label gives you, what that protein costs, how the coach model works now, and whether it is worth it.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Shakeology Products Ranked</a>
      <a href="#protein">How Much Protein</a>
      <a href="#ingredients">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Is It Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#mlm">Is It Still an MLM?</a>
      <a href="#meal-or-protein">Meal or Protein Powder?</a>
      <a href="#buyers">What Buyers Say</a>
      <a href="#vs-rivals">Shakeology vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Shakeology is a low-protein superfood shake at a high price: 16 to 17 g of protein per serving, per BODi, for $129.95 per 30 servings, which is about $6.40 per 25 g of protein at full price, four to six times what plain whey costs. Sales and the new 0g Added Sugar line change the maths a little. This review covers the label, the testing question, the coach sales channel and who should still buy it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Shakeology Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shakeology is a decent superfood shake and a poor-value protein powder. Each serving gives 16 to 17 g of protein, per BODi, alongside fiber, probiotics, enzymes, vitamins and a long list of plant extracts. At the $129.95 list price for 30 servings, that protein costs about $6.40 per 25 g, against roughly $1 to $1.70 for ordinary whey. Holiday sales cut that to around $3.75, which is still more than double.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy it if you want one daily shake that bundles protein, fiber and greens, you value BODi's workout programs and community, and the price does not matter much to you. Skip it if your goal is protein for muscle, a budget, or verified third-party testing, because a plain protein powder plus a piece of fruit gives more protein for a fraction of the cost. If you do buy, choose the 0g Added Sugar line and start with a small bag.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Shakeology at a Glance (BODi, Nov 2026)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">16-17 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per serving</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$129.95</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">List price, 30 servings</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~$6.40</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cost per 25 g protein at list</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">None</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Named certification found</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Shakeology Products, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            The four Shakeology options worth knowing in November 2026, ranked for a buyer who cares about protein and sugar. Figures are per BODi or the sources named, at the time of writing, and vary by flavor. BODi sells Shakeology on its own site, so these cards link to a retailer search rather than a specific listing.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Protein Is in Shakeology?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shakeology has 16 to 17 g of protein per serving, depending on flavor, per BODi's product page. That is less than most protein powders, which usually give 20 to 30 g per scoop, and less than the 25 to 30 g that research on muscle building tends to use per meal. BODi's own blog compares it with about three large eggs, which is a fair description of the dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein source depends on the version. Whey flavors use whey isolate, a filtered milk protein that is high in leucine and low in lactose. Vegan flavors use a blend of pea, quinoa, flax and rice protein. Both are complete enough for most people when the rest of the diet is varied, and our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein guide</a> explains how the two compare for training.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason the protein is modest is that a Shakeology scoop is shared with other things. Healthline's review put a serving at 36 g of powder for 140 to 160 calories, and BODi says each serving carries at least 6 g of fiber plus probiotics, enzymes, vitamins and superfood powders. In a typical whey isolate, 25 g of protein takes up most of a 30 g scoop. In Shakeology, protein is under half of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a smoothie, you can lift the protein by blending Shakeology with milk or soy milk rather than water, which adds roughly 7 to 8 g of protein per cup, or by adding Greek yogurt. That is a sensible fix, but it also tells you something: if you need to add a second protein source to reach a normal serving, the shake is working as a nutrition supplement rather than a protein powder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Shakeology Besides Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shakeology is protein plus a long superfood and supplement blend: fiber, a probiotic, digestive enzymes, vitamins and minerals, and plant extracts including adaptogens. BODi's product page lists Bacillus coagulans at 2 billion CFUs, vitamin C, calcium, iron and potassium, and superfoods such as ashwagandha, maca and matcha green tea. Older BODi material also names chlorella, goji, pomegranate, rose hips, reishi mushroom and bilberry.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweetening differs by line. BODi's blog describes the reformulated original as sweetened with organic cane sugar plus stevia and monk fruit, and BODi has long said the shake contains no artificial sweeteners, flavors, colors or preservatives. Healthline's 2022 review put most original flavors at 7 g of total and 5 g of added sugar. The 0g Added Sugar line removes the cane sugar; published reviews say it relies on stevia and monk fruit. Anyone avoiding stevia should look at our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">protein powders without stevia</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem with superfood blends is dose. The ingredient amounts for most of the plant extracts are not shown individually, so you cannot tell whether a serving contains a meaningful amount of maca or matcha or just a sprinkle. That makes it hard to credit the shake with the effects of any single ingredient, and it is the same issue we raise with most proprietary greens blends.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ashwagandha deserves its own caveat. It is an adaptogen with some evidence for stress and sleep, but it is not recommended in pregnancy, can interact with thyroid, sedative and immune-suppressing medicines, and has been linked to rare cases of liver injury. If you are pregnant, breastfeeding, on prescription medicine or have a thyroid or liver condition, ask your doctor before drinking Shakeology daily. The matcha may also add some caffeine; BODi does not state an amount on the page we read.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Does Shakeology Cost per 25 g of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the $129.95 list price, Shakeology costs about $6.37 per 25 g of protein, and at the November 2026 sale price of $76.67 it costs about $3.76. The arithmetic: 30 servings of 17 g is 510 g of protein, or 20.4 portions of 25 g. If your flavor has 16 g per serving, the list-price figure rises to about $6.77. A serving costs $4.33 at list price and about $2.56 on sale.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price basis</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g protein</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shakeology (list price)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">16-17 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$129.95 / 30 servings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $6.40-$6.80</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shakeology (Nov 2026 sale)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">16-17 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$76.67 / 30 servings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3.75-$4.00</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Herbalife Formula 1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies by mix</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Our Herbalife review</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $5.70</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ka'Chava</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Our Ka'Chava review</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $4.00-$4.70</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical whey or Premier powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Retail</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.00-$1.70</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Against the obvious alternatives, Shakeology is the most expensive protein on the list at full price. Our <a href="/herbalife-protein-powder-review/">Herbalife review</a> puts Formula 1 at about $5.72 per 25 g at US retail, and our <a href="/kachava-review/">Ka'Chava review</a> puts that meal shake at about $4 to $4.70. Both of those are also premium products. A mainstream whey or the Premier Protein powder costs roughly $1.20 to $1.70 per 25 g, so the full-price Shakeology premium is four to five times.
          </p>

          <p>
            BODi runs frequent sales, and in November 2026 the site showed a Cyber Sale with discounts of up to 47% on Shakeology, limited to the first month of a subscription or a one-time purchase. Subscriptions renew at the same term and price, less applicable discounts, and can be cancelled anytime, per BODi. Read the renewal terms before you buy, because the sale price usually applies only once, and the second bag is at the regular price.
          </p>

          <p>
            BODi offers a 31-day money-back guarantee, less shipping and handling, which excludes final-sale items and subscriptions started through a free trial. That is a useful safety net for a first bag. The site also says it accepts HSA and FSA payment, which can lower the effective cost for eligible buyers, though whether a food product qualifies depends on your plan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Shakeology Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            BODi says Shakeology is third-party lab verified, but it does not name a certification, and we found no NSF Certified for Sport, NSF Contents Certified, Informed Choice, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project listing for it at the time of writing. That is a meaningful distinction: a brand testing its own product with outside labs is not the same as an independent body certifying the product and publishing its status.
          </p>

          <p>
            BODi's supplements page lists clinically tested benefits and third-party lab verification among Shakeology's selling points, and its product page refers to industry-leading quality assurance. A coach blog repeats a claim of more than 1,500 tests per batch, but that is not a BODi document and does not say what is tested. Healthline's 2022 review noted that Beachbody products were not certified by third-party organisations, though the company said it tests for safety and quality.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are the question most buyers mean. Plant-heavy powders, especially those with cocoa, rice protein and greens, tend to carry more lead and cadmium across the category than plain whey, and Shakeology contains all three in some versions. BODi did not publish heavy-metal results or certificates of analysis on the pages we read. You can ask BODi customer service for a recent certificate of analysis; a brand confident in its testing should be able to provide one.
          </p>

          <p>
            If certification matters to you, choose a product with a public listing. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder guide</a> lists powders you can check in the NSF database yourself, and our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains how to read testing claims and what to ask a brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mlm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Shakeology Still Sold Through an MLM?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No, not in the old sense. The Beachbody Company, now BODi, announced on September 30, 2024 that it was ending its multi-level marketing network and moving to a single-level affiliate program from November 1, 2024, with the MLM structure fully wound down by January 1, 2025. Executive chairman Mark Goldston described the MLM model as outdated and unsustainable, and the company cut about a third of its workforce alongside the change.
          </p>

          <p>
            For years Shakeology was sold mainly through Team Beachbody coaches, who earned commission on their own sales and on sales made by people they recruited. That structure is why so many Shakeology reviews online are written by coaches, and why so many people met the product through a friend's social media posts. Under the affiliate model, people still earn commission on sales they refer, but there is no recruiting downline.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a buyer, the practical effects are small but worth knowing. You can buy direct from BODi's website without going through anyone, and the price is the same either way. A friend who recommends Shakeology may still earn a commission on your order, so weigh enthusiastic personal reviews accordingly. And many reviews that still rank in search results were written by coaches under the old model, which is why we lean on the label and independent sources here.
          </p>

          <p>
            The MLM history does not make the shake itself bad. It does explain the pricing: the product was built to support multiple layers of commission, and the list price is still $129.95 for 30 servings. If you like BODi's workout programs and community, buying from them is reasonable. If you only want the nutrition, you are paying for a brand and a business model you do not need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="meal-or-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Shakeology a Meal Replacement or a Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shakeology sits between the two, and it is not quite enough of either on its own. At about 130 to 160 calories and 16 to 17 g of protein with water, it is too light to replace a meal for most adults, and it has too little protein per scoop to be an efficient protein powder. BODi pitches it as a daily dose of dense nutrition, which is the most accurate way to think about it.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a meal replacement, most people need to blend it with milk, fruit and a fat source such as nut butter to reach 300 to 400 calories and 25 g or more of protein. That works, and the fiber helps with fullness, but at that point you are building a meal around a $4 base. Our <a href="/best-meal-replacement-shake/">best meal replacement shake</a> guide compares shakes designed to stand in for a meal on calories, fiber and cost per meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            For weight loss, Shakeology can be one tool among many. Replacing a higher-calorie breakfast with a 150-calorie shake creates a calorie deficit, and structured meal replacements have some evidence behind them. But there is nothing specific to Shakeology in that effect; any low-calorie, fiber-rich shake would do the same. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">weight loss protein powder guide</a> lists cheaper options with more protein per calorie.
          </p>

          <p>
            For muscle gain, use a dedicated protein powder. You would need two Shakeology servings to match one standard whey scoop, at roughly $8.66 at list price, against about $1 to $1.50 for the whey. If you like the greens and fiber, a cheaper approach is a basic protein powder plus a separate greens or fiber supplement, and you keep control of each dose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buyers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Do Buyers Say About Shakeology Taste and Digestion?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyer reviews of Shakeology are split, and price is the complaint that comes up most. We have not tasted Shakeology; everything here is reported feedback. BODi's product page shows a 4.7 rating on its own site, but brand-hosted ratings and coach-written reviews skew positive, so we weighted independent review sites and forums more heavily.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chocolate is the flavor buyers most often name as their favorite, in both whey and vegan versions, and reviewers often recommend blending it with ice or banana rather than shaking it with water. On independent review sites such as ChickAdvisor, critics describe the texture as chalky unless blended, and some find the chocolate too sweet or notice the stevia. Healthline's review also noted chalkiness when not blended thoroughly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Digestion is mixed. Some buyers say Shakeology settles their stomach or helps with regularity, which fits its fiber and probiotic content. Others report gas, bloating or cramps in the first weeks, which Healthline also summarised from online reviews. Six to seven grams of fiber in one drink is a jump for people who eat little fiber, so starting with half a serving for a week is a sensible approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other common complaints are about value and fullness. Several reviewers say the price is a dealbreaker for monthly use, and some say a shake made with water does not keep them full until the next meal. Praise centres on convenience, the habit of one daily shake and the feeling of getting greens in without effort. The Cinnamon Roll flavor launched in November 2026 and has no meaningful buyer feedback yet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Shakeology vs Herbalife, Ka'Chava and Plain Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against its closest rivals, Shakeology has the least protein per serving and, at list price, the highest cost per gram of protein. Ka'Chava gives 25 g of plant protein per serving at about $4 to $4.70 per 25 g, per our review, with a similar superfood and fiber pitch. Herbalife Formula 1 is the other MLM-era shake, at about $5.72 per 25 g at US retail, and it has been sold through distributors far longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ka'Chava is the most direct comparison because it is also a plant-forward superfood meal shake with adaptogens, fiber and probiotics, sold direct to consumers. It has more protein per serving and costs less per gram, though it is still a premium product. Huel is another option if you want a full nutrient profile per serving at a lower cost; our <a href="/huel-protein-powder-review/">Huel review</a> covers its lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Herbalife shares Shakeology's history: both were built on networks of distributors or coaches. Herbalife still sells through independent distributors. Our <a href="/herbalife-protein-powder-review/">Herbalife protein powder review</a> covers Formula 1 and Formula 3 in detail. Other brands with a similar sales history include <a href="/arbonne-protein-powder-review/">Arbonne</a> and <a href="/shaklee-protein-powder-review/">Shaklee</a>, which we have also reviewed from the label.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plain whey is the comparison that matters for anyone buying Shakeology for protein. A standard whey gives 24 to 25 g of protein in about 120 calories for roughly $1 to $1.50 per 25 g. Add a banana and a spoon of oats and you have more protein, similar fiber and a fraction of the cost. You lose the probiotics, adaptogens and greens, which matter more to some people than others.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Who Should Buy Shakeology, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Shakeology if you want one daily shake that combines moderate protein with fiber, probiotics and greens, you are happy to pay a premium for convenience, and you already use BODi's workout programs. In that case choose the 0g Added Sugar line, start with a 7 or 14-serving size, and use the sale price and the 31-day guarantee to test whether you actually drink it every day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Shakeology if your goal is protein for muscle or recovery, because you can buy protein for a quarter of the cost. Skip it if you want verified third-party testing, if you are pregnant or breastfeeding, or if you take medication that interacts with ashwagandha, unless your doctor approves it. Skip the original line if you are cutting added sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most readers, the better value is to split the job: a plain protein powder for protein and a cheap fiber or greens product if you want those, or a purpose-built meal shake if you want a meal. Our <a href="/best-meal-replacement-shake/">meal replacement shake rankings</a> compare the main options on calories, fiber and cost per meal, and our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein powder guide</a> covers dairy-free protein at a far lower price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating it as a full protein serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At 16 to 17 g of protein, one Shakeology serving is about two thirds of a standard protein scoop. If you are counting it toward a 25 to 30 g post-workout or breakfast target, blend it with milk or Greek yogurt, or use a dedicated protein powder instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a subscription at the sale price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                BODi sales often apply to the first month of a subscription only, and subscriptions renew at the same term and price, less any discounts. Note the regular $129.95 price, set a reminder before the second shipment, and cancel if the shake is not part of your routine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing it with water and expecting a meal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                With water, Shakeology is roughly 130 to 160 calories, which is a snack, not a meal. People who use it as breakfast and get hungry by mid-morning are usually under-eating. Blend it with milk, fruit and a fat source, or eat a real meal alongside it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the ashwagandha</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The original formula lists ashwagandha among its superfoods. It is not recommended in pregnancy and can interact with thyroid, sedative and immune-suppressing medicines. Check the label of the flavor you buy and ask your doctor or pharmacist if you take regular medication or have a liver or thyroid condition.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting coach reviews as independent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                For more than a decade, Shakeology was sold mainly through coaches who earned commission, and many reviews still ranking online were written by them. Affiliates still earn commission on referrals. Read the label, compare cost per 25 g of protein, and look for independent reviews before deciding.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Shakeology third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  BODi says Shakeology is third-party lab verified, but it names no certification, and we found no NSF, Informed Choice, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project listing at the time of writing. BODi does not publish heavy-metal results on its product pages. You can request a recent certificate of analysis from customer service. If certification matters to you, choose a product listed in the NSF or Informed databases.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Shakeology healthy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a reasonable daily shake: 16 to 17 g of protein, at least 6 g of fiber, probiotics, vitamins and plant extracts, per BODi, with no added sugar in the 0g line. It is not a complete meal with water, and it contains ashwagandha in the original formula. Healthy does not mean good value; it is an expensive way to get those nutrients.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Shakeology have sugar or artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  BODi says Shakeology contains no artificial sweeteners. The original formula uses organic cane sugar plus stevia and monk fruit, and a 2022 Healthline review put most flavors at 7 g total and 5 g added sugar. The 0g Added Sugar line has no added sugar and, per published reviews, uses stevia and monk fruit. Check the panel for your flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Shakeology flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Chocolate is the flavor buyers most often name as their favorite, in both whey and vegan versions, and reviewers often recommend blending it with ice or banana rather than shaking it with water. We found too little independent feedback to rank Vanilla, Café Latte, Tropical Strawberry or Cookies & Creamy, and Cinnamon Roll is brand new. We have not tasted any of them.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the common complaints about Shakeology?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Price is the most common complaint by far, with many buyers calling $129.95 a month a dealbreaker. Other frequent complaints in buyer reviews are a chalky texture unless blended, sweetness or stevia aftertaste, gas or bloating in the first weeks, and not feeling full with water. Praise centres on convenience and making a daily greens habit easy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Shakeology still an MLM?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. BODi ended its multi-level marketing network in favor of a single-level affiliate program from November 1, 2024, and wound down the MLM structure by January 1, 2025. Former coaches can still earn commission on sales they refer, but there is no recruiting downline. You can buy directly from BODi at the same price without going through anyone.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Shakeology worth it for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help as a low-calorie breakfast or snack replacement, because a 130 to 160-calorie shake with fiber is filling for its calories. But nothing in Shakeology causes weight loss that a cheaper high-protein, high-fiber shake would not. If cost matters, a plain protein powder with fruit gives more protein for less. Speak to a doctor if you have a medical condition.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

